--TEST--
a `?T` property and a `?T` parameter each take a written `null` default — `null` is a constant of a type that admits one, and the decoder is the same for both positions — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:classes/no-undefined-value`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Row {
    // `rule:classes/no-undefined-value` exempts a nullable property from definite assignment,
    // because nullability already promises "may legitimately hold no value".
    // A written `= null` states that at the declaration rather than leaving it
    // to the exemption, and it is the one default such a property obviously
    // has -- so refusing it refused a constant of the declared type.
    public ?int $rank = null;
    public ?string $note = null;
    public static ?string $last = null;

    // The neighbouring rows, so this case says an arm was added to the grid
    // rather than the grid being loosened: a non-nullable property still takes
    // only a constant of its own type, which
    // `a-property-default-that-is-not-a-literal-is-a-compile-error.nvst` pins
    // from the refusing side.
    public int $id = 7;
    public string $name = "ada";
}

Row $row = new Row();
echo $row->id, " ", $row->name, "\n";
echo $row->rank ?? -1, " ", $row->note ?? "<none>", "\n";
echo Row::$last ?? "<none>", "\n";

class Ask {
    // The parameter half of the same decoder. A default is evaluated once at
    // signature collection and emitted by the *caller*, so `null` has to be a
    // constant the call site can materialize exactly as `-1` is; both calls
    // below reach one compiled arity.
    public static function rank(?int $given = null): int {
        return $given ?? -1;
    }

    public static function note(?string $given = null): string {
        return $given ?? "<none>";
    }
}

echo Ask::rank(), " ", Ask::rank(3), "\n";
echo Ask::note(), " ", Ask::note("hi"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
7 ada
-1 <none>
<none>
-1 3
<none> hi
